  "Question": "Let \\(f\\) be a function for which \\(f\\left(\\dfrac{x}{3}\\right) = x^2 + x + 1.\\) Find the sum of all values of \\(z\\) for which \\(f(3z) = 7.\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-\\dfrac13\\)",
    "B": "\\(-\\dfrac19\\)",
    "C": "\\(0\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac59\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac53\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "To use the given rule for \\(f(3z)\\), the input \\(x/3\\) must equal \\(3z\\). Set \\(x=9z\\), giving\n\\[\nf(3z)=(9z)^2+9z+1.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(f(3z)=7\\) exactly when\n\\[\n(9z)^2+9z-6=0.\n\\]\nTreating \\(9z\\) as a single quantity makes the factorization straightforward:\n\\[\n(9z+3)(9z-2)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero, so the only solutions are \\(z=-1/3\\) and \\(z=2/9\\). Both give valid real inputs. Their sum is\n\\[\n-\\frac13+\\frac29=\\boxed{-\\frac19},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
